window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][132] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A teammate suggests using an AI tool to write the PRD and summarize the user research for you. What do you think, and what is your approach?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Great, it saves time,&quot; or &quot;I wouldn't use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names what it is good for and what it is not, with verification.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes drafts from evidence, protects confidential data, keeps the decision and the research with people, labels the work, and gives a concrete workflow.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <code>[as of 2026-10]</code> I'd use it for speed on drafts and structure, and I'd be strict about what counts as evidence. A model's output is a draft or a hypothesis. It is not a source. <em>For the PRD.</em> I'd write the problem, the goal and the non-goals myself, because they are my judgment, and give the tool my bullet points to turn into a first draft, to suggest missing sections, to list edge cases and to argue against the plan (a red team). Then I'd edit hard: check every number, every claim about users and every technical statement against a source, and remove generic text that says nothing specific. <em>For the research.</em> The evidence is the participants and the transcripts (C06). I could use a tool to organize notes I give it, to propose themes and to find quotes, but I'd check each theme against the transcripts and quote only what a person actually said. A summary is a pointer to the transcripts, not a replacement for them. And a model-generated &quot;user&quot; is not a user: its answers say what text on the internet looks like, not what my customers do. <em>Data and privacy.</em> I'd follow the company's policy: no customer data, unreleased plans or personal information in tools that are not approved for them. For approved tools, I'd still strip identifiers when I can. <em>Ownership and honesty.</em> The decision, and the accountability, stay with me and the team. I'd say in the document where AI helped and not present synthesized text as findings. If the PRD contains a claim I cannot trace to a source or my own data, it comes out. <em>What I'd tell my teammate.</em> &quot;Great for drafting and for finding gaps. For anything we rely on, we verify against a source, and the research stays with real users.&quot; What would change my practice: a tool approved for confidential data and with traceable citations to the underlying sources, in which case I'd still verify the citations.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> It is the day before launch. QA finds a bug that loses user data for 0.5% of users, with a three-day fix. Leadership wants to ship on time. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ship and fix it later,&quot; or &quot;delay.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Classifies the severity, sizes the harm and weighs the delay.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Applies a pre-set release rule, considers staging and its limits (staging still exposes some users), identifies alternatives that remove the harm without delay, and communicates clearly with a go or no-go owner.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> Data loss is an S1 under any sensible release rule: it harms users irreversibly. My starting position is no-go for a full launch until it is fixed or contained, and I'd check that the rule agreed in advance says the same (&quot;no open S1&quot;). <em>Size the harm.</em> With 200,000 users in the first month (invented), 0.5% is 1,000 people losing data. Even a staged release does not make that safe: at 5% exposure, 50 people could lose data, which I would not accept as a planned outcome. The harm is not recoverable by a patch after the fact. <em>Size the delay.</em> A three-day delay costs something real: say $15,000 a day of expected revenue and a commitment to partners (invented), $45,000 and some reputational cost. That is small beside 1,000 people's data and the trust cost. <em>Look for ways to contain without delaying.</em> (1) Can the bug be avoided by disabling the feature path that triggers it, via a feature flag, and launching the rest? (2) Can we launch to users who do not hit the affected condition, with a safe fallback? (3) Can the fix be done in less than three days, or the launch moved by one day with partial fixes verified? (4) Can we warn and offer a backup before the risky action? Any option that removes the data-loss path is acceptable; one that merely reduces it is not. <em>Decision and communication.</em> Recommend: hold the launch for the fix, or launch with the risky path disabled if that is feasible and verified. Tell leadership the facts in five lines: what the bug does, how many users it could hit, the options with cost and date, my recommendation, and the go or no-go owner (me with the engineering lead, with the sponsor informed). Tell partners and support the new date or the reduced scope. <em>Afterward.</em> Add a regression test for the case, and look at why testing found it late. What would change my mind: evidence that the affected condition is much rarer than assumed or that the data can be recovered automatically.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What does &quot;ready to launch&quot; mean to you?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The code is done and tested.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Lists criteria across product, reliability, data, support and constraints, with a decider.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Scales the gate to the stakes, requires a rehearsed rollback and written success criteria, notes that only the decider may waive a criterion and in writing, and links readiness to a staged plan.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> Ready means a short list of criteria is met, each with an owner, and one named person has decided go. A date is not readiness. <em>The criteria.</em> Product: acceptance criteria met, defects triaged by severity, no open S1, accessibility checked. Reliability: load tested, monitoring and alerts live, and a rollback or off-switch rehearsed, because a rollback that has never been tried is a hope. Data: events logged and verified in production, a dashboard and a baseline, and success criteria written before launch. People: support trained with scripts, sales and success informed, internal and external messages ready. Constraints: legal, privacy and security sign-offs recorded and any open risk accepted in writing by a named owner. Rollout: stages, dwell times and rollback triggers agreed. <em>The decider.</em> One person, usually the PM with the engineering lead, makes the go or no-go call at a meeting a few days before the date, with the checklist in front of them and dissent recorded. <em>Scale it.</em> A copy change behind a flag needs a light gate; a payments change needs the full one. And I'd write the criteria in week one so the date does not bend them in week six. A criterion may be waived only by the decider, in writing, so the exception is visible and rare. The mistake to avoid is &quot;launch Friday&quot; as the goal. If testing finds problems on Thursday, the right move is a smaller launch or a later date, chosen by the criteria and not by the calendar.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is a feature flag, and why should a PM care?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It turns features on and off.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains staged exposure, fast rollback and experiments.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Covers canary and ramp use, kill switches, testing in production with a small audience, the cost (complexity, flag debt) and what a PM does with them.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> A feature flag is a switch that turns functionality on or off for chosen users without shipping a new release. The code for the feature is deployed, but who sees it is controlled by the flag. <em>Why a PM cares.</em> First, <em>staged rollout</em>: I can show a change to staff, then 1% of users, then 10%, and widen only if it behaves, which limits the blast radius. Second, <em>fast rollback</em>: if something goes wrong, switching the flag off takes seconds, compared with an hour or more to redeploy. Third, <em>experiments</em>: flags assign users to variants, so I can compare an exposed group with a control (C14). Fourth, <em>decoupling release from launch</em>: engineering can ship code when it is ready and the business can choose the moment to turn it on, with marketing and support aligned. Fifth, a <em>kill switch</em> for risky components. <em>Costs.</em> Flags add complexity: more code paths to test, and flags that stay after a launch become debt, so each flag needs an owner and a removal date. A flag is not a replacement for testing: it limits the damage of a mistake, it does not prevent it. <em>What I do with them.</em> Agree with engineering which launches use flags, who can flip them, the stages and the rollback triggers, and make sure the off-switch is tested before launch. Where a change cannot be flagged (a data migration, a hardware design), I treat it as higher risk and gate it harder.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A test of a checkout change shows orders up 0.4%, but the page loads 80 milliseconds slower. Do you launch?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, orders are up,&quot; or &quot;No, latency matters.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs the gain against the cost of latency using the data.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures the product's own latency sensitivity, computes the net under stated assumptions, considers fixing latency and staged launch, segments by device, and sets a decision rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd not decide on the sign of each number. I'd ask whether the 0.4% survives the latency, and I'd use our own data for the cost of slowness. <em>A rough check.</em> A widely cited claim from the mid-2000s is that each additional 100 milliseconds of latency cost a retailer about 1% of sales <code>[V: Greg Linden, Make Data Useful, Stanford talk slides, 29 November 2006: 100 ms extra load time, 1 percent drop in Amazon sales; cited by Kohavi and Longbotham, 2007; the size today is not established]</code>. That figure is old and context-specific (Kohavi's own later slides describe it as pages slowed by 100 to 250 milliseconds costing about 1% of revenue, so scaling it in a straight line is itself an assumption), so I'd treat it only as a prompt for an assumption. If it held here, 80 milliseconds costs about 0.8% of orders. Net: +0.4% − 0.8% = −0.4%. On that assumption, launching loses orders. If our sensitivity were only 0.25% per 100 milliseconds, the cost is 0.2% and the net is +0.2%. So the answer depends on a number I can measure. <em>Measure the sensitivity.</em> Run an experiment that adds artificial delay to a random group (say +100 ms) and measure the change in orders. That gives our own cost per 100 milliseconds, by device (mobile users on slow connections are often more sensitive). <em>Look for a better option.</em> Why is the page slower? If the cause is fixable (an uncompressed image, a blocking script), fix it and retest: the 0.4% gain without the latency may be the real result. If the slowdown is inherent, ask whether the gain justifies it on the segments where it is positive. <em>Decision.</em> Do not launch to everyone on this readout. Fix the latency and retest, or launch in stages to the segments (desktop, fast connections) where the net is positive, with the latency guardrail watched and a holdout. The rule: launch if orders are up by more than the latency cost, with the interval for the net above zero. What would change my mind: a measured sensitivity much lower than the claim. <em>Company note.</em> Amazon is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that launch prompts weigh conversion gains against latency and reliability <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Amazon data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Two days after launch, sign-up conversion has dropped 3%. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Roll back,&quot; or &quot;investigate.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Confirms the drop is real, relates it to the launch, and decides on rollback with the cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the pre-set triggers, applies the diagnosis steps, separates launch effect from other causes, decides with numbers, and communicates.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as an incident with a diagnosis attached. First, is it real and is it ours? I'd apply step zero from C13: is the data complete, is 3% outside the normal swing (say daily swings are ±1.5%, so 3% is notable), and does the timing line up with the launch (the drop starts at the release hour and not before)? If the launch was staged, compare the exposed group with the unexposed: a drop only in the exposed group is strong evidence the launch is the cause; if both dropped, look for an external cause. <em>Check the trigger sheet.</em> If a rollback trigger was set (&quot;sign-up conversion down more than 3% over a rolling 24 hours&quot;), and it is hit, the action is pre-agreed: pause the ramp or turn off the flag, then diagnose. If no trigger existed, I'd decide with numbers. <em>The numbers.</em> With 10,000 sign-ups a day, a 3% drop is 300 sign-ups a day. Suppose each is worth $10 in lifetime margin (invented): $3,000 a day. The launch must have been expected to add more than that to be worth keeping. If the benefit is not yet visible, or the drop is concentrated in a segment, rollback or a partial rollback is cheap relative to waiting. <em>Locate.</em> Segment by platform, browser, country and new versus returning users (C13.5): if one browser accounts for most of the drop, the cause is likely a bug and the fix is quick. If the drop is spread evenly, it could be the design itself, and an experiment is the right tool. <em>Decide and communicate.</em> If the drop is confirmed in the exposed group and the cause is not quickly fixable, roll back or reduce exposure to 10% while diagnosing. Tell stakeholders the five lines (what, since when, what we know, what we are doing, next update). What would change my mind: the drop disappearing when the data finishes arriving.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A hardware accessory has a software defect found two weeks before manufacturing locks. Decide.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Delay and fix it,&quot; or &quot;ship and patch.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Recognizes irreversibility, sizes the defect and weighs options by cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Tests whether the fix-later path is real (including any re-certification), uses a gate that preserves the option to delay, quantifies expected cost, considers supply and support, and treats safety as separate.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the situation only in general terms; nothing here describes any real product. The first question is what kind of defect: if it can hurt someone, safety decides and it is not a tradeoff; I'll assume it is a functional defect. <em>Classify.</em> The manufactured parts are irreversible after the lock; the firmware is changeable only through an update path. So the question is whether the fix-later path is real. I'd ask three things. Can the fix be delivered over the air, to units affected by the defect? Does the fix need regulatory re-certification, and how long does that take? What is the exposure window before the fix reaches customers? <em>Numbers (invented).</em> The first run is 400,000 units. The defect affects 2.5% in lab tests: 10,000 units. A field problem costs about $90 per affected unit (support, returns, replacement): $900,000. Suppose the fix requires a three-week re-certification before it can be released. In the first three weeks, 40% of volume sells, 160,000 units, of which 2.5% are affected: 4,000 units and $360,000 of field cost in the window. Delaying the lock by 10 days would miss the main selling window and cost an estimated $1,500,000. <em>Options.</em> (A) Lock on time; start the re-certification now so the fix is ready within about three weeks; mitigate the defect in the meantime with a documented workaround and a support script; and run a gate: by day 7, verify that the fix works on affected units and that the update path reaches them. (B) Delay the lock 10 days: $1,500,000 for sure. (C) Lock on time and ship as is: $900,000 of field cost, plus the reputational cost. <em>Compare.</em> If the gate passes (say probability 0.75), expected cost is the window cost, $360,000. If it fails (0.25), I delay the lock by the remaining time at, say, $750,000. Expected: 0.75 × $360,000 + 0.25 × $750,000 = $270,000 + $187,500 = $457,500. That beats B ($1,500,000) and C ($900,000). The gate preserves the option to delay while buying the one piece of evidence that decides. <em>Also.</em> Check that the first units out can be flagged and updated before shipping; size support; and decide who may decide at the gate. What would change my mind: the defect turning out to be safety-related, or the update path failing in the lab on day 2, in which case delay immediately. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that hardware prompts at Apple include defects found near manufacturing deadlines <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real product, process or numbers.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your launch date is fixed and engineering says the remaining work will take three times longer than planned. Five other teams must deliver pieces. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Work harder,&quot; &quot;add people,&quot; or &quot;push the date.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Looks at scope, dependencies and the critical path, and negotiates scope.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes compounding dependency risk, orders scope cuts in advance, preserves the launch criteria, proposes a staged launch on the date, and communicates early with options.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> With a fixed date and a threefold gap, the lever is scope or exposure, not effort. I'd do four things this week. <em>Understand the gap.</em> Ask engineering for the range and the riskiest part (C16.4), and check against the history of similar projects. Separate what is truly three times longer from what can be descoped. <em>Map the dependencies and compound them.</em> Five other teams, each, say, 90% likely to deliver on time: 0.9⁵ = 59%. Even before the threefold estimate, the plan had a 41% chance of slipping on dependencies. I'd find the critical path, raise the weakest items (build against mocks, agree fallbacks, book reviews now), and cut the count on the path. <em>Decide the order of cuts, in advance.</em> First polish and nice-to-haves; then secondary markets and features; never the launch criteria (quality bar, safety, accessibility, privacy). The smallest launch that delivers the core of the user's job, perhaps a third of the scope, becomes the plan for the date. The rest follows in a second release with a date. <em>Use staging.</em> Launch on the date to a smaller group or one market, with the remaining scope behind flags to be enabled as it is ready. That meets the date in a meaningful way without exposing everyone to unfinished work. <em>Communicate.</em> Go to the sponsor now with options and costs, not on the eve of the date: &quot;At full scope we will miss by about X weeks; for the date we can ship the core to one market; adding people helps little at this stage (the arithmetic from C15.4); here is my recommendation.&quot; What I'd avoid: promising the full scope on the date, asking engineers to cut testing, or hoping. What would change my mind: a dependency owner offering to pull in their delivery.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> It is 2 a.m. You are the PM on call. An alert shows that 5% of users cannot complete a core action since last evening's release. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Wake up the engineers and fix it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Confirms impact, contains by rollback, communicates, then diagnoses.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses pre-agreed triggers and decision rights, runs the PM's parallel tracks (impact, communication, decisions, log), weighs rollback against fix forward, and sets up a blameless postmortem.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> My job is not to debug; it is to make sure the right people are working the problem, the impact is limited, and everyone who needs to know does. <em>First 15 minutes (FW-C17-06).</em> (1) <em>Confirm impact:</em> who, how many, since when, how severe? Is the alert real (a second source)? Five percent of users failing at a core action is severe. (2) <em>Contain:</em> the release was last evening; if it was behind a flag, the on-call engineer can turn it off, and the trigger sheet probably says to. I'd confirm that and not debate. If it cannot be flagged, decide quickly between a rollback and a fix forward: rollback is usually safer if the previous version is known good and the rollback is rehearsed. (3) <em>Communicate:</em> an internal status in the incident channel with the five lines, and a plan for the customer message and the status page if it will last past the morning. Tell support what to say. (4) <em>Decide the tradeoffs the engineers bring:</em> for example, disabling the new feature entirely for 100% of users or only for the affected 5%; the PM is the person who weighs customer impact and scope. (5) <em>Log:</em> keep a timeline. <em>Protect the team.</em> Provide one voice upward with a status every 30 to 60 minutes, so the engineers working the fix are not interrupted by questions. <em>After containment.</em> Diagnose with the C13 sequence (what changed, who is affected, timing, location, size), confirm that metrics recover, and decide whether to relaunch with a fix and a smaller ramp. <em>Postmortem.</em> Within a few days, a blameless review: timeline, root causes, why testing and monitoring did not catch it earlier, what went well, and actions with owners and dates (for example, an alert on the core action's success rate, a canary stage). I'd own the product-impact and decision sections and follow up the actions. What would change my mind: if the rollback itself is risky (a data change), in which case a targeted disable and fix forward may be better, and I'd say so with the reasoning.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is strategy, and how is it different from a plan or a goal?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's the long-term plan to achieve goals.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines strategy as choices based on a diagnosis, distinct from goals and plans.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives the kernel (diagnosis, guiding policy, coherent actions), includes what is declined, and offers a short example and a test for bad strategy.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> A goal is where you want to end up. A plan is a list of steps and dates. A strategy is the logic that connects them: a diagnosis of the situation, an approach chosen to deal with it, and actions that reinforce each other. The distinguishing feature is choice, and especially what you decide not to do. An example. A clinic-scheduling product says: &quot;Grow 40% and launch mobile, analytics, integrations and an AI assistant.&quot; That is a goal and a project list. A strategy would read: &quot;Diagnosis: 70% of our revenue comes from small clinics, who churn in the first 90 days because setup is hard, while large clinics need integrations we cannot deliver. Policy: win small clinics by being the easiest to set up; decline large-clinic integrations and new markets this year. Actions: guided setup, a calendar import tool, referral through clinic associations, one simple price.&quot; The actions all push in the same direction, and the declined items are named. A quick test for a real strategy: can you say what the company will not do, and why? If every initiative fits, it is a wish list. Another test: would the plan look the same under any strategy? Then it expresses none. At my level, strategy shows up as understanding the company's choices and connecting my product's decisions to them, and as the ability to reason about a strategic question when it arises.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Estimate the size of the market for a dog-walking app in a city of 1.2 million households.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> One estimate with no check.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Two routes (top-down, bottom-up), a range, and a so-what.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains the gap between routes, checks against a reality constraint, separates total from obtainable, and states the swing assumption and a way to test it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll estimate two ways and compare. <em>Top-down.</em> 1,200,000 households; suppose 25% own a dog: 300,000. Say 10% use a paid walker regularly: 30,000 households. At 3 walks a week for 52 weeks at $20 a walk: 30,000 × 3 × 52 × $20 = $93.6 million a year. <em>Bottom-up (supply side).</em> Suppose 2,000 active walkers in the city, each doing 6 walks a day, 5 days a week, 50 weeks a year, at $20: 2,000 × 6 × 5 × 50 × $20 = $60 million a year. <em>Compare.</em> The top-down is 1.6 times the bottom-up. A likely reason: the top-down adoption (10% of owners using a walker, 3 walks a week) is optimistic, while the bottom-up counts only walkers who are currently active, and informal walkers (neighbors, friends) are not counted in either. So the current paid market is probably nearer the lower figure. <em>Reality check.</em> A walker doing 6 walks a day, 5 days a week, earns $600 a week, about $30,000 a year: plausible for a full-time walker. Cross-check demand against supply: 30,000 households at 3 walks a week is 90,000 walks a week; at 30 walks per walker per week that needs 3,000 walkers, 1.5 times the 2,000 assumed. That is the same 1.5 gap, so the demand assumptions (adoption and frequency) are the likelier overstatement. <em>Range and so-what.</em> $60 to $94 million for all paid walking; I'd plan on $60 million. Our obtainable share is the question: with a 5% share in three years, about $3 million in gross bookings; an app taking a 20% commission would earn about $600,000 in revenue, which may not support a team. So the size sets up the real question: what commission and share, and whether the app is more than a directory. The assumption that swings it is paid-walker adoption (10% could be 4% or 15%), which a small survey or a pilot could test.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Should a device maker launch a subscription bundle of its services?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, bundles increase revenue,&quot; or &quot;no, it will cannibalize.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Diagnoses the situation, models the economics, and addresses cannibalization.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names ecosystem integration as the source of advantage, computes the break-even on cannibalization and the retention benefit, states the assumption to test, and proposes a staged test.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general: I know the product only from public information, and nothing here describes any real plan. I'd first ask what the company wants (more revenue per customer, lower churn, a stronger ecosystem) and diagnose the current state: how many customers pay for how many services. <em>Diagnosis (invented).</em> Per 1,000 users: 250 pay for two or more services (average $22 a month), 450 pay for one ($8), and 300 pay for none. A bundle of four services at $20 is cheaper than buying them separately. <em>Where the advantage comes from.</em> Ecosystem integration: the services work better together and with the device, and customers who use several are less likely to leave. A bundle reinforces that and makes switching costlier (C18.5). <em>Economics.</em> Three effects. <em>Cannibalization:</em> the 250 multi-service payers move to $20 and lose $2 each: −$500 a month per 1,000 users. <em>Upgrades:</em> single-service payers who add the others at +$12 each; suppose 5% do: 450 × 0.05 × $12 = +$270. <em>New payers:</em> non-payers who join at $20; suppose 3%: 300 × 0.03 × $20 = +$180. Net: −$500 + $270 + $180 = −$50 a month per 1,000 users: roughly break-even on the first month. With 8% upgrades and 4% new payers: +$172. So on revenue alone the bundle is marginal; its value depends on adoption. <em>The retention benefit.</em> If bundle subscribers churn less, say 2.1% a month against 3.0% (invented), the expected lifetime rises from 33 months to 48 months. For a multi-service payer, $22 a month over 33 months is $733; $20 over 48 months is $952: a gain of about $219 per customer in lifetime revenue, in spite of the lower monthly price. So the bundle pays if the retention effect is real, and it does not if it is not. That is the assumption to test. <em>Other effects.</em> Pricing power: a bundle makes it harder to raise individual prices; each service's team may lose visibility of its own economics; regulators may examine bundles that tie services together <code>[V: the European Commission's 2022 decision on Amazon's Prime and Buy Box practices is an example]</code>. <em>Recommendation.</em> Test it before committing: offer the bundle in one market for a quarter, with a holdout, and measure upgrades, new payers and, most importantly, retention of bundle subscribers against a matched group. Launch more widely if retention improves by enough to give a positive lifetime value (at least a 20% longer lifetime in this example). I would change my mind if the retention gain were small, in which case the bundle is a discount. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that strategy prompts at Apple include ecosystem and services decisions <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real bundle, price or data.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A company has a popular free, ad-supported consumer product. Should it launch a paid, ad-free tier?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, subscriptions are good revenue.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes the revenue gained against ad revenue lost, and notes who would pay.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Recognizes selection (heavy users are worth more in ads), sets the break-even, considers the effects on the free product, positioning and trust, and proposes a test.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I know such products only from public information, and nothing here describes a real plan. The crux: the people who would pay to remove ads are the people the ads earn the most from. <em>Numbers (invented).</em> 1,000,000 monthly users; average ad revenue $2.40 per user per month. A paid tier at $8. Suppose 4% convert: 40,000 payers, $320,000 a month. If the converters were average users, the ad revenue lost is 40,000 × $2.40 = $96,000, a net of +$224,000. But paying users are typically heavier users: if their ad value is $5 a month, the loss is $200,000, a net of +$120,000. The break-even is where the converters' ad value equals the price: if they are worth $8 a month in ads, the tier gains nothing. So the key estimate is the ad value of the converters, which I can measure from usage. <em>Other effects.</em> Does the paid tier change the free product (fewer heavy users sharing content, a weaker network)? Does it affect advertisers (a smaller audience)? Does it change how users see the company (a paid product has to be better; trust is higher when the customer pays)? Is there a segment (professionals) that would pay for features beyond ad removal, which would justify the price better? <em>Alternatives.</em> A lower-priced tier; feature-based paid tiers; a usage limit on the free tier. I'd evaluate the paid tier on the heavy segments first. <em>Recommendation.</em> Run a limited test: offer the paid tier to a sample of heavy users, measure conversion, the ad revenue they leave, retention and any effect on others, and compute the net per converter. Launch if the net per converter is clearly positive and retention is higher among payers. What would change my mind: converters' ad value near $8. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that strategy prompts at Google concern monetizing free consumer products <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or plan is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How should a platform owner respond if regulation opens alternative app distribution?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Fight it,&quot; or &quot;comply and accept lower revenue.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies what is at stake (revenue, security, quality, developers, users) and lays out response options.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Models revenue under different shifts, separates what regulation requires from what the owner chooses, considers second-order effects on security and developers, and proposes a staged response with measures.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> This is an Illustrative scenario, with regulatory facts as of 2026-10 <code>[V: Apple Form 10-Q, 2026, on US and EU proceedings; the EU Digital Markets Act]</code>; I know the area only from public information, and nothing here describes any real company's plans. Several jurisdictions have adopted or considered rules that require large platforms to allow other app stores or payment methods. I'd treat the question as how to respond, in order. <em>Diagnosis.</em> What is at stake: (1) <em>revenue</em>: the platform earns a commission on developer billings; (2) <em>security and quality</em>: the platform's review and distribution control is part of its promise to users; (3) <em>developers</em>: they want lower fees and direct relationships; (4) <em>users</em>: they value safety, simplicity and choice; (5) <em>the ecosystem advantage</em>: integration between devices and services (C18.5). <em>Revenue under shifts (illustrative index).</em> Developers bill 100 through the platform; commission 30%: revenue 30. Suppose regulation allows alternatives, and the platform charges a reduced technology fee of 5% on billings that move. If 10% of billings move: 90 × 30% + 10 × 5% = 27 + 0.5 = 27.5, down 8%. If 30% move: 70 × 30% + 30 × 5% = 21 + 1.5 = 22.5, down 25%. The size of the shift is the swing assumption, and it depends on how much developers gain by moving and how much users will follow. <em>Options.</em> (A) Contest the rule and comply minimally. (B) Comply fully and compete on value: invest in the quality of the platform's own store and services so that users and developers choose it. (C) Comply and redesign the commercial terms (a lower commission with a fee for services such as payments, review, discovery, and updates) so that the platform's revenue tracks the value it provides. (D) Comply with strong safeguards (verification, notarization, clear warnings) for alternative channels. <em>Evaluation.</em> A delays and risks penalties and goodwill. B and C aim to keep users and developers on the platform by merit. D protects users from the new risks (malware, scams) that alternative channels can create, a real second-order effect. The options combine: C and D are compatible, and B is the long-term goal. <em>Second-order effects.</em> Security incidents on alternative channels could harm users and the platform's reputation even if the platform is not at fault; developers may fragment their releases; competitors may exploit the opening; privacy promises may be harder to keep. Regulators may scrutinize the new terms themselves. <em>Recommendation.</em> Comply in good faith, restructure commercial terms to charge for value (C), build the safeguards (D), and invest in making the platform's store the best place to distribute (B). Measure: share of billings that move, security incident rate, developer satisfaction, user trust, and revenue per user. Stage by region where rules apply. I'd change my mind if the share of billings moving were small, which would mean less urgency, or large, which would call for a deeper rethink of the model. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only (a platform owner of the kind Apple and Google are): <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that strategy prompts at Apple include platform and regulatory scenarios <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any company's actual response, fees or rules.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> An enterprise software company has three business lines: a mature suite that funds the company, a fast-growing platform, and an experimental line. How do you allocate investment?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Put the most money in the growing business.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Assigns each line a role and criteria, and allocates with reasons.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Protects the cash source, sets stage gates for the experiment, uses numbers on return, names interdependencies between lines, and states what would change the allocation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I know such companies only from public information. The first move is to give each line a role, because the right investment depends on what the line is for. <em>Roles (invented figures).</em> The <em>mature suite</em>: revenue 60 (index), growth 3% a year, margin 45%. Its role is to fund the company and to defend against erosion. The <em>growth platform</em>: revenue 30, growth 25%, margin 30%. Its role is to become the next major business and to be the company's future. The <em>experimental line</em>: revenue 2, growth 100%, margin deeply negative. Its role is to find out whether a new bet is worth scaling. <em>Criteria.</em> Return on the strategic goal, size of the opportunity, strength of the advantage, risk, and interdependence (does one line strengthen another?). For example, the platform may sell to the suite's customers, and the suite's relationships may be its distribution. <em>Allocation logic.</em> Suppose 10 units of investment capacity. <em>Suite:</em> enough to defend and modernize, since it produces 27 of profit (60 × 45%) and any erosion hurts everything; say 2. <em>Platform:</em> the largest share, since growth at 25% on 30 is 7.5 of new revenue a year, with a good chance of becoming the largest line; say 6. <em>Experiment:</em> a bounded amount with stage gates, say 2, released in stages against milestones (customer evidence, unit economics) with a stop rule. <em>Numbers to check.</em> The platform's growth adds 7.5 in revenue at 30% margin, about 2.25 of profit a year at current margins, rising as scale grows; the experiment may have a low chance of success but a high payoff. The suite's decline rate matters: if it fell by 5 a year, that would be worth more than a small platform gain, so I'd check what the suite needs to hold its position. <em>Interdependencies.</em> If the platform's customers come through the suite's relationships, cutting suite investment harms the platform. I'd map such dependencies. <em>Decision and review.</em> Allocate about 2, 6 and 2, review each quarter, and shift if the platform's growth slows, if the suite shows faster erosion or if the experiment reaches a gate. What would change my mind: evidence that the experiment is more promising than its stage suggests (a larger share), or that the suite's customers are at risk of leaving (more to defend). <em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that portfolio-allocation prompts at Microsoft involve mature, growth and experimental lines <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or plan is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-07</h3>\n";
